Staged build through 'dump hdf5'
================================

Three SPPARKS invocations build one microstructure across overlapping
windows, each seeding itself from the previous stage's file:

  stage 1   x = [0, 100)     seeds itself, creates the file
  stage 2   x = [80, 180)    overlaps stage 1; grows the datasets to x=180
  stage 3   x = [40, 140)    overlaps both

This is the pattern real AM decks use (compare examples/am/legacy/stitch/am_demo,
which does the same thing through 'dump stitch').  The point of the
example is that the whole staged workflow -- read back, continue the
clock, extend the domain -- works with a format that keeps only the
latest value per site.

Each later stage does:

  set        site value 7                              # fresh material
  set        site hdf5 hdf5_staged.h5 skip_novalue     # previously built
  reset_time hdf5 hdf5_staged.h5                       # continue the clock

'skip_novalue' is what keeps the two apart.  Sites the file has never
covered come back as the dataset fill value (-1 for integer fields), and
skip_novalue leaves those at whatever the preceding 'set' put there --
here the value 7, standing in for unmelted powder.  Sites the file does
cover overwrite it.  This is the same contract 'set stitch
... skip_novalue' provides.

Note there is no 'first'/'last' argument: an hdf5 dump holds one state,
not a history, so there is nothing to select between.

Running
-------

Requires a build configured with --highfive.  The comparison half of
run.sh additionally needs --package stitch.

  ./run.sh                 # or: NP=8 SPPARKS=/path/to/spk ./run.sh
  python3 compare.py       # needs h5py

run.sh performs the same three stages twice, once through 'dump hdf5'
and once through 'dump stitch', and compare.py checks that the two agree
site for site.  They should match exactly: both backends copy the same
bytes out of the same per-site arrays.

What to expect
--------------

compare.py reports the two files' sizes.  The hdf5 file is several times
smaller here, and the gap widens with the number of dumps: stitch appends
every dump to the file, while the hdf5 datasets are overwritten in place,
so their size is set by the domain alone.

Use 'dump stitch' instead when the time history matters -- movie frames,
'set stitch <file> first', or reading an intermediate time.  None of
those are recoverable from an hdf5 dump.
